# @@NAME@@

R3 robot project. `robot.yaml` indexes the complete local robot description.
Part metadata lives in `parts/<id>.yaml`; electrical and software descriptions
live in `electrical/system.yaml` and `software/system.yaml`. For example:

```yaml
components:
  link: {$include: parts/link.yaml}
electrical: {$include: electrical/system.yaml}
software: {$include: software/system.yaml}
```

Includes replace a complete value and use project-relative paths. The pipeline
resolves them into one spec for every stage. Model parts, mate connectors and
mates directly in Onshape; R3 reads a pinned local CAD copy under `cad/pulled/`.
Git does not preserve read-only bits: after checkout run `find cad/pulled -type f -exec chmod a-w {} +`;
the pipeline still verifies every pinned checksum and derived description field. CI restores these permissions.
Part `pulled` fields and root `attachments` are owned by pull and must not be
edited. `r3_get_spec` returns every source file; `r3_patch_spec` routes changes
to their owning files and requires the SHA-256 of the complete description.

R3 validates the description, electrical design, wiring and deployable software
in a multi-domain digital twin.

## Quick start

```bash
python -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
pip install "r3-platform[sim,mcp] @ @@PLATFORM_URL@@@@@PLATFORM_REF@@"
r3 validate            # all stages, writes build/report/index.html
r3 diagnose            # errors grouped by component / wire / binding with hints
r3 sim run --scenario @@FIRST_SCENARIO@@
```

Open `build/report/index.html` for diagnostics, scenario results and plots.

## Agents

Codex and Claude Code read `AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md`. The `r3` MCP server is
registered in `.mcp.json` for Claude Code; for Codex add to `~/.codex/config.toml`:

```toml
[mcp_servers.r3]
command = "python"
args = ["-m", "r3_orchestrator.mcp_server"]
env = { R3_PROJECT = "/path/to/@@NAME@@" }
```

## CI

`.github/workflows/r3.yml` validates every push and pull request inside the
R3 worker image (KiCad, ngspice, MuJoCo, Freerouting; fully headless), uploads
`build/` as an artifact and posts a job summary. Optional secrets:
`R3_WORKER_IMAGE_TOKEN` (private worker image) and `R3_PLATFORM_TOKEN` (private
platform repository). Run `r3 doctor` to see which stages can run headless.

## Deploy

`r3 deploy --target sim` runs the software against the twin.
`r3 deploy --target fleet --yes` deploys to the robots through BLITZ
(`backend/deploy.py`); it refuses unless the current `robot.yaml` validated
without errors. Install the BLITZ development backend into this project first
(see the BLITZ README) so `backend/deployment/` exists.
